WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.930
- Greetings everyone I'm Saad Omer,

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:10.580
I'm the director of Yale
Institute for Global Health.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.180
I'm really delighted to welcome you

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:15.870
to the Yale Institute for Global
Health Conversation Series.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.920
In addition to Yale
Institute for Global Health,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.970
which is also called YGH, we are indebted

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:24.107
to the George Herbert
Walker, Jr Lecture series

00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:27.950
and international studies
at the MacMillan Center

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.530
for International and Area Studies at Yale

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:34.990
for of course sponsoring
this event and the series.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.760
Today's conversation will be focused

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.760
on the impact of COVID-19
pandemic on higher education.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.910
We'll cover different aspects of it,

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.530
but we will specifically discuss

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:49.200
the pandemic response strategies

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.280
taken by university
presidents around the world.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.020
We will also consider how the pandemic

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.920
has shaped the future of higher education.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.190
And so obviously they, it was a little bit

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.760
of a baptism of fire for
several institutions,

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.600
it was challenges that institutions faced

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.200
but out of these challenges
come opportunities

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.950
and changes for the longterm.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.610
And so this is an
opportunity to touch base

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.500
with three prominent university leaders

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.010
to discuss some of those things

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.640
that will be different going forward.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.890
It is my pleasure to have therefore

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.170
three distinguished
guests to join us today.

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:31.090
All of whom are esteemed leaders

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.210
in higher education around the globe,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:37.240
Peter Salovey, who's the
president of Yale University

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.190
here in Connecticut, Betsy Bradley

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.510
president of Vassar College in New York

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.023
and president Firoz Rasul,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.360
president of the AGA Khan University

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.930
which is headquartered
in Karachi, Pakistan

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:54.290
but has campuses in
many parts of the world.

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.260
Before moving to our
question and discussion,

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.520
I will introduce each of our guests

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.063
in a little bit more detail.

00:02:05.389 --> 00:02:08.780
Peter Salovey is the the 23rd president

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:13.780
of Yale University and a
professor of psychology

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.850
he has held several
leadership positions at Yale

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:19.270
since becoming president in 2013,

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:24.210
he has led the development of
new programs and facilities

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.440
strengthened partnerships worldwide,

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.770
increased access to
Yale College Education,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:33.770
which is a remarkable goal and achievement

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.264
for a university

00:02:36.264 --> 00:02:39.140
and enhanced multi-disciplinary
collaborations

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:43.000
and entrepreneurial opportunity
for faculty and students.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.790
During the COVID 19 pandemic,

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:48.900
president Salovey has
been leading Yales efforts

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.970
to control the spread of the
virus and mitigates its impact.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.140
Yale scientists and scholars
are conducting research

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:01.140
on treatments, tests and
vaccines for this virus

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.930
and helping people understand the science

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:06.670
behind the public health guidance

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:09.640
and collecting and analyzing
data to address issues

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.950
like unemployment and vaccine reluctance.

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.340
So it's been a 360 degree response

00:03:16.693 --> 00:03:21.693
from a university that has
multiple areas of excellence.

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.300
Betsy Bradley has served as the president

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.490
of Vassar College since 2017,

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.070
in that time she has led the college

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.730
to establish new programs
and partnerships in India,

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.120
Rwanda, and China to bring the model

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.400
of liberal arts, higher
education to these settings

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.420
and in many parts of the world.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.250
And most recently she
served on New York state's

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.700
reopening advisory committee

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.272
and help draft the guidelines for New York

00:03:50.272 --> 00:03:54.200
higher education reopening
in the midst of the pandemic.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.300
And so it was a pretty daunting task

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.770
for university leaders to figure out

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:03.770
how to accomplish their
educational and research missions.

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:06.900
And I think New York was
lucky to have someone

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.900
with Betsy's background
as a university leader,

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.550
as an educational leader in the state.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:19.550
Prior to coming, prior to
becoming the president of Vassar,

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:22.520
president Bradley was
on the faculty at Yale

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.270
for more than 20 years

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.480
she was elected to the National
Academy of Medicine in 2017

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.270
and is a member of the
council of Foreign Affairs.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:35.820
The third esteemed guest
is president Firoz Rasul

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:39.200
he has served as the president
of the Aga Khan University

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.210
since 2006, under his leadership,

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.210
the university has grown geographically,

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:48.570
but also programmatically
in terms of its impact

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.250
and diversity, AKU

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:53.140
which the Aga Khan
University is known popularly

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:57.450
as now operates in Kenya,
Tanzania, Uganda, Pakistan

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:01.370
Afghanistan, and the UK
through its six campuses,

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:06.370
seven teaching hospitals,
300 outreach medical centers

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.774
treating more than 2
million people each year.

00:05:09.774 --> 00:05:11.570
AKU was teaching research and healthcare

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:16.110
has been transformative
and under his leadership

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:20.980
during the COVID-19 pandemic,
AKU earned its position

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:24.990
as a trusted partner for
two national governments

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.200
for innovation and health
and education policies

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.920
emergency response, and
clinical trials for vaccines

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.250
and therapeutics.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:36.560
For example, AKU hospitals
were first in Pakistan

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.493
and Kenya to treat a COVID patient

00:05:39.493 --> 00:05:42.960
to offer molecular testing

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.800
and to administer vaccines
on behalf of governments

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.090
where AKU is placed,

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:50.280
so, it's my pleasure to welcome
all three of our guests.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:55.070
I'll start the first round
with a big picture question

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.410
Peter, what was the
biggest challenge you faced

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:04.083
as a university president
and how did you respond?

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.500
- Well, thank you, Saad

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:09.090
and it's a pleasure to be here

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:12.750
and a pleasure to be
with so many people today

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.163
with fellow presidents Firoz and Betsy,

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:20.173
really an honor for me.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.150
And I think it's very
important to emphasize

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:27.230
before I answer the question that while I

00:06:27.230 --> 00:06:31.200
maybe the president of
Yale, the team working

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:36.200
on our COVID response was
enormous and committed.

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:42.750
And Saad for example, was on a
call every morning at 7:00 AM

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:48.540
as part of our health, our
public health policy group

00:06:48.760 --> 00:06:52.100
by really helping us understand the data

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.330
and make campus policy
on the basis of science

00:06:55.330 --> 00:07:00.330
and data and many, many many pitched in.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:04.330
Our biggest challenge in many
ways I think is the challenge

00:07:04.330 --> 00:07:08.780
of every college and university president.

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.650
And that is to do two things

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:17.830
a massive pivot that
required a multifaceted

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.640
changes across the whole university

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.940
while not giving up on the idea

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:29.350
that we are gonna continue
our mission, education

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:31.563
and research primarily.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:37.460
So as we started to hear
about the novel coronavirus

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:41.450
last winter, but before
it came to this country

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:45.360
or to our region, we were already planning

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:50.350
on how do we keep the community
and our neighbors safe.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:52.810
And I have to say that is

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:55.580
the single biggest challenge, right?

00:07:55.580 --> 00:07:58.110
How do we make sure that our university

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:02.740
doesn't become a vector
for the disease in a way

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.230
how do we make sure our
local community is safe

00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.770
but that we're not spreading this disease

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:13.770
through our whole city and
our region and beyond, right?

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.940
It's an enormous responsibility.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:18.410
And then how do we continue education

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:21.203
and research while doing so?

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.650
So I would say the biggest challenge

00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:29.600
and I'm speaking at a
pretty abstract level here

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:33.060
is balancing these two objectives,

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:37.520
staying safe, keeping the community safe

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.270
keeping ourselves from
spreading the virus,

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:43.220
beyond the walls of the campus

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:45.250
while fulfilling our responsibilities

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.073
as a major research university.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.370
And as a research university, we knew

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.410
that we would need to rely on our experts.

00:08:54.410 --> 00:08:55.760
Our experts in public health,

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:57.673
our experts in medicine and nursing.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.620
We knew that we'd get insights

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:05.370
from people in student affairs
and housing and dining.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.130
Just give a couple of examples.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:11.270
We knew that we needed
to quickly put together

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.820
a public health policy group
that I mentioned earlier

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:18.330
an Emergency Policy Group
to implement recommendations

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:19.760
from the Health Policy Group

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:25.610
and people all over the
campus really responded.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:29.730
So within a couple of months,

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:34.730
well within what a week and a
half of sending everyone home

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.853
essentially for spring break,

00:09:39.800 --> 00:09:43.970
we were able to pivot to substantially

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:48.250
an online learning environment.

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:51.320
This was thanks to our faculty

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.430
who are willing to learn
new skills very quickly

00:09:54.430 --> 00:09:56.820
and thanks to a great teaching

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:59.030
and learning center called
the Poorvu Center who

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:02.493
provided coaching and technical
assistance and instruction.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:08.530
And very quickly, we were
able to reopen laboratories

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:12.100
especially the ones
doing research relevant

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.420
to COVID, it's transmission,

00:10:14.420 --> 00:10:16.020
it's treatment, it's prevention.

00:10:17.290 --> 00:10:20.380
But I would say perhaps the most important

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:22.060
and this will be my final comment

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:25.399
I'm speaking a little
too much at length here

00:10:25.399 --> 00:10:28.950
are the most important component

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:32.000
to being able to stay
committed to our mission

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:35.730
making sure that we were teaching,

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.710
making sure that our
researchers were advancing ways

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:41.690
to fight COVID-19
vaccinations, treatments,

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:43.850
innovations in patient care.

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:47.810
The most important single
thing that happened

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:49.980
on this campus so far

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:58.560
was that our students and
others adhered to the guidelines

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:02.770
people wore masks,
people social distanced,

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:05.860
people stopped gathering in groups

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.330
and that happened immediately.

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:11.003
And it has been sustained for 14 months.

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:15.600
The strongest warnings that we heard

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:19.363
was that particularly
students would not do that.

00:11:20.495 --> 00:11:21.837
And you could not trust
your students to do that

00:11:21.837 --> 00:11:24.620
and you were there were
editorials being written

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:27.780
that it was unfair for
university presidents

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.270
to put that burden on students.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:31.770
Undergraduates in particular

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:34.500
or late adolescent young adults

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.960
they couldn't be trusted to do this.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.060
And we made the decision

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.710
that we were gonna trust
our students to do this,

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:42.580
to do the right thing.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:44.130
We're gonna make it as easy as we could

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:44.987
for them to do the right things

00:11:44.987 --> 00:11:47.010
and we were gonna be as clear

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:51.080
as we could communicating
what needed to be done.

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.300
But at the end of the day

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.260
we were gonna trust our students,

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:56.850
I don't want to be
president of a university

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:00.880
where I can't trust our
students to do the right thing.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:02.290
And I'm glad we did.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:05.850
It may have been the single
most important component

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.640
of all this to answer the question,

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:10.530
the part of this that was the single

00:12:10.530 --> 00:12:12.900
most specific challenge.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:17.900
And I'm just grateful and
proud that our students

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:22.500
and our community as a
whole did the right thing

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:25.000
and continues to do the right things

00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:26.963
in response to the threat of the virus.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.760
- Excellent, before I
go to the next speaker

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:32.730
I would remind that
people have the ability

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:36.450
to sort of ask questions
that we will sort of channel

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:39.963
towards our speakers,
our panelists in a while,

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:43.920
after we sort of get through
the first part of the section

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:48.220
a lot of you are following are
watching it on Facebook live.

00:12:48.220 --> 00:12:53.220
And so there's a way to send
questions through that as well

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:57.800
and so I appreciate
everyone staying engaged.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:01.010
Betsy you're leading an institution

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.923
that had a few unique
assets, it's location,

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.710
it's student body

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:11.210
and a president who had a
public health background

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.370
any specific insights, again,
in terms of the big picture

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:16.990
that the biggest challenges you've faced

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:20.557
and anything you you'd like to share

00:13:20.557 --> 00:13:24.700
that university leaders can
learn from your experience

00:13:24.700 --> 00:13:27.540
and your unique vintage point.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:28.373
- Thank you, Saad,

00:13:28.373 --> 00:13:29.470
I really appreciate being invited

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:31.470
and Peter was so nice to imagine

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.140
and think back to what Yale
was like so congratulations

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:36.020
to everything you've
been able to accomplish

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:38.493
in these last treacherous 14 months.

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:41.410
Just maybe a little bit of orientation.

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:46.300
We have about 2,500
undergraduates at Vassar

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:50.680
a faculty of three to 400, 300
people, about 1200 employees.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:55.020
And we stayed open through the
whole pandemic and still are.

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:58.180
So we have about 85% of
our students are on campus

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:01.390
taking classes in person
as many faculty wanna

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:03.910
do some kind of hybrid or
there are students remote

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:06.550
so, and we have a ton of tents around

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:09.080
so, ultimately depending on the weather

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:12.970
the classes are 50/50
in person or not remote

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:15.250
in the fall it was more like 70% in person

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:16.220
when it was nice and sunny.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.320
And I'm hoping to see that again, this May

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.050
so it's been quite a ride
and I think looking forward

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:26.060
and looking back the
question you had posed to us

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:28.540
what is the biggest challenge?

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:30.400
I actually think there are a couple

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.420
that happened really early on.

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:34.830
The first biggest challenge was to screw

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:37.940
up the courage to say, we're coming back.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:40.550
And I remember the exact
senior team meeting

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:43.300
when I could see a path

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:46.480
towards how we could actually
house students on campus.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:48.440
And I was working with the New York State.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:51.610
So I knew it would be, by the guidelines.

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:53.570
But I remember saying to people,

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:57.100
look we have to decide now
we're coming back or we're not

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.880
you can't decide that
in a month and a half.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:00.990
It has to be now.

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:03.040
And we're gonna have to
plan every single minute

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.420
for the next eight weeks
to be able to do this.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:08.930
And our team like said, okay, let's do it

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:10.750
so that was a brilliant moment.

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:13.180
And then I would say the second thing

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:15.450
that occurred is very soon after that

00:15:15.450 --> 00:15:18.360
is we set our values.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.490
We had a long meeting
about what were our values

00:15:21.490 --> 00:15:23.400
and our values ultimately were

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.440
to protect the most vulnerable among us.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:26.900
So no matter what we did

00:15:26.900 --> 00:15:28.930
we were going to either
the financially vulnerable

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:32.380
or the medically vulnerable,
or maybe isolation vulnerable

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:35.410
socially vulnerable, that was job one.

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:36.930
And our second value was equity.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.100
Everything we did, we
tried to do equitably.

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:41.740
So if we're gonna allow
the athletes to do this

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:44.060
we had to allow the rest of
the non-athletes to do that.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:47.370
In other words, the
context would be one where

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:50.190
we could be as equitable as possible

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:52.420
and in all of campus response.

00:15:52.420 --> 00:15:54.010
And then like Peter, and we said

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:55.860
our third value is our mission.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.570
We provide the highest
quality liberal arts education

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:01.530
and inclusive and diverse setting.

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:04.310
And we're gonna keep that mission alive.

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:05.950
So setting those values

00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:09.180
ended up being fundamental
to everything we did.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:11.383
And then I would say the largest challenge

00:16:11.383 --> 00:16:14.623
which was similar to what
Peter said was culture change.

00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:18.670
How do you get 2,500 students to decide

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:21.110
yeah, they're gonna be
not socializing with

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:22.960
each other the way they used to

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:25.470
not gonna eat indoors with
each other for awhile,

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:27.367
all of the various pieces.

00:16:27.367 --> 00:16:29.130
And how do you calm down the parents

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:31.620
who are very nervous about
the fact you're gonna do this.

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:35.330
And that culture change really
came through a mechanism.

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:37.060
We call the Vassar Together

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:40.140
which was about a 40 person committee

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:42.930
of faculty, students,
staff, administrators

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:45.040
coming up with what
would the guidelines be?

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:46.590
And we tried to use harm reduction

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:47.990
what were guidelines we could live by

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:49.993
what were things we would say,

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:53.820
we can actually hold
everybody on this campus

00:16:53.820 --> 00:16:54.800
and if we do that

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.900
then maybe they don't need
to be so careful masking

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:58.980
because they're not
getting exposed outside

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:00.220
once we know they're negative.

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:03.880
And honestly that worked,
it was and still works.

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:06.960
I mean, even now that we're
more vaccinated than we were

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.170
we're constantly looking
back at these to say, okay,

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:11.870
well can people go into town now

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:16.650
or can we have class inside
of the normal density

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:20.950
all of those now go through
the Vassar Together group.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.560
And if there is any sort of

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:26.530
obligation of the expectations,

00:17:26.530 --> 00:17:31.220
we also have a large group
called CCT that actually

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:33.500
reeducate, does restorative justice,

00:17:33.500 --> 00:17:36.460
does do some referral to
student conduct if there issues

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:38.860
so, those structural pieces that we put in

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:40.890
I think they were challenging.

00:17:40.890 --> 00:17:42.990
I guess the culture
change was the challenge.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:44.630
And I think those structural pieces really

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:47.390
helped us achieve it at least so far.

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:49.720
But look I'm still learning everyone else

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:51.570
so don't tell me to teach others.

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:54.120
I wanna hear what Firoz
and Peter have to say

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:55.680
and learn from that so we can

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:57.280
continue to be healthy and safe.

00:17:58.150 --> 00:17:59.190
- Great, wonderful.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:03.390
So Firoz, AKU has this unique
role in multiple countries

00:18:04.370 --> 00:18:08.590
as the premier sort of
healthcare, teaching institution

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:11.590
and also as increasingly
in other fields as well

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.990
and especially in education
and media, et cetera

00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:17.530
any thoughts from that perspective

00:18:17.530 --> 00:18:20.650
that the biggest challenges that you faced

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:24.113
as a university president
and how did you respond?

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:27.800
- Thank you very much
Saad for this invitation

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:32.550
and it's fascinating
listening to Peter and Betsy

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:37.270
to see here their experiences
in the last 14 months.

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:38.400
As you pointed out,

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:40.130
we are in multiple countries.

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:43.120
And the first thing that
we had to figure out

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.700
for each of our countries,
what the government was doing

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:50.980
and remember our governments
are all in the developing world

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:53.580
and they initially were not sure

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:55.160
about whether they should lock down

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:58.370
whether they should allow
continued engagement

00:18:58.370 --> 00:19:00.518
or interaction of people.

00:19:00.518 --> 00:19:04.560
And they were sending out
signals on what schools

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:07.980
and universities should
do, public transportation

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:12.910
and so long lock downs on
shopping and other areas.

00:19:12.910 --> 00:19:16.210
And the lock downs were much
more onerous than anything

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:18.620
I think we've seen in the US or in Europe.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:21.410
And so we were trying to navigate our way

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:25.240
through that to say, how do
we keep the institution going

00:19:25.240 --> 00:19:27.750
while we try and work with the rules

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:30.980
and regulations from the local governments

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:33.900
and even and even those
rules were changing

00:19:33.900 --> 00:19:37.100
almost by the week as
they were learning more,

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:39.120
as all of us were learning more.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:40.490
So that was our first challenge

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:42.850
in trying to figure out what we could

00:19:42.850 --> 00:19:45.370
and could not do in each of the countries.

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:47.513
Borders were closed so we have

00:19:47.513 --> 00:19:51.617
movement between countries was curtail.

00:19:51.617 --> 00:19:55.220
And that also affected our
faculty who used to travel

00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:58.550
between campuses or within the country.

00:19:58.550 --> 00:20:01.240
So that was the first
thing we had to adjust to.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:04.900
The second thing that
I would say that being

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:07.130
as an international university

00:20:07.130 --> 00:20:11.150
is how do you now come
up with a set of common

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:13.830
goals or values, as we've heard

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:16.740
that we are going to
adhere to and practice

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:20.090
while complying with national

00:20:20.090 --> 00:20:22.820
in multiple countries requirements.

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:25.210
And so that was a big challenge for us.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:28.550
And we had to figure out
how to do that and one way

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:32.170
I guess it was more by the
invitation of the government

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:35.650
than by any deliberate strategy by us

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:40.650
was we were appointed on
all of the advisory groups

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:42.360
that each government had set up

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:46.310
to help them make policy
decisions through this process.

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:48.920
So we had our faculty, our researchers,

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:52.090
our administrators on several
committees with governments.

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:56.040
And so we were able to
influence shape or inform,

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:58.120
look if you do this, then
this is the consequence,

00:20:58.120 --> 00:21:01.670
if you do that, then
maybe we can adjust this

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:04.350
and be more flexible in some areas.

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:06.210
So that was the second challenge.

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:09.970
And I think the third
for us as a university

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:13.090
because we are a private,
not for profit university

00:21:13.090 --> 00:21:15.082
in the developing world,

00:21:15.082 --> 00:21:20.082
most of our income that we
support other than tuition

00:21:20.410 --> 00:21:24.320
comes from either donations
or comes from the income

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:29.220
of our hospital, the seven
hospitals that you mentioned

00:21:29.220 --> 00:21:31.980
around the countries where we are,

00:21:31.980 --> 00:21:34.910
and immediately what happened
was as COVID came about

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:39.620
the non COVID patients
disappeared, they just evaporated.

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:42.130
And that was our income

00:21:42.130 --> 00:21:43.780
and we need to figure out

00:21:43.780 --> 00:21:47.080
how are we gonna keep
going without the flow

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:52.080
of funds coming in from our
hospitals and healthcare system.

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:54.840
And that was a big shock
to the whole system.

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:58.223
And I think Peter used
the word massive pivot,

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:00.650
I think massive is an understatement,

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:02.950
I mean, the pivot that
we had to figure out

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:06.710
is how do we not only keep
going with the education side

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:09.760
but also how do we continue
to run the hospitals?

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:13.510
Because people wanted care,
they wanted to come for tests,

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:16.660
they wanted to know whether,
this fever they have

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:20.140
or this cold they have is
not something more serious.

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:22.580
And I remember one day in Karachi

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:25.700
our hospital was full emergency was full

00:22:25.700 --> 00:22:27.990
and we had a group of 2000 people outside

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:29.200
trying to get into the hospital.

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:32.220
And we had to get the
police to stop the people

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:35.580
from rushing the campus and
the campus at the hospital

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:40.480
is also where our academic facilities are.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:42.100
So the students couldn't go in and out

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:43.900
people couldn't go in and out.

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:48.321
And this panic set in about,
what's gonna happen to me.

00:22:48.321 --> 00:22:50.770
And of course it affected our staff

00:22:50.770 --> 00:22:54.960
and it affected many of our faculty

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:58.210
and doctors and nurses who
were all trying to provide.

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:00.800
So we were on the one hand trying to teach

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:03.120
and keep that going, on the other hand

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:07.418
trying to make sure that
we kept our facilities open

00:23:07.418 --> 00:23:09.940
for those people who needed care.

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:11.610
And thirdly was trying to figure out,

00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:14.890
well how do we actually
get some funds out of this?

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:16.690
And that was our biggest challenge

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:19.900
and it continues to be, although
some of that is stabilized

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:22.430
we've figured out how to separate between

00:23:22.430 --> 00:23:25.390
our facilities, our
campuses, our hospitals,

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:27.640
those patients that come for COVID

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:29.880
or COVID testing or COVID treatment.

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:33.020
And those patients that come
for non COVID health care

00:23:33.020 --> 00:23:35.130
and how we can continue to
give them the confidence

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:36.900
that we will keep you safe,

00:23:36.900 --> 00:23:39.540
you will get treated for heart disease

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:42.950
or diabetes or cancer,
whatever else you have,

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:46.210
and that we will help you continue

00:23:46.210 --> 00:23:48.630
to get recovery from your disease

00:23:48.630 --> 00:23:51.360
and make sure you're not
gonna be infected with COVID.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:53.400
So for instance, every
patient that comes in

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:55.710
we test them for COVID just to make sure

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:58.841
that they're not carrying
something asymptomatic.

00:23:58.841 --> 00:24:00.920
And then we know we're dealing
with a different problem.

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:03.170
And so we figured out a protocol

00:24:03.170 --> 00:24:05.600
a system where we're able to do this.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:07.449
And the last thing I would say

00:24:07.449 --> 00:24:09.890
before we open it up to questions

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:13.090
is we set up a global response team,

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:16.140
we call it the GRT, we meet once a week.

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:18.450
We used to meet once every two weeks

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.060
probably go back to once a week,

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:21.850
depends on the we're on the third wave

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:23.530
in our countries right now

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:25.370
so we will have to adjust.

00:24:25.370 --> 00:24:26.990
And the biggest challenge
that we're facing,

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:30.200
not just the fact that we have
increasing number of patients

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:33.500
but what are we gonna
do about vaccinations?

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.650
You mentioned that we are one of the few

00:24:35.650 --> 00:24:37.680
government vaccination sites

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:40.430
but you're talking about very few vaccines

00:24:40.430 --> 00:24:42.980
because most of the vaccines

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:45.000
that come to the countries where we are

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:47.630
will come from Covax or this facility

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:51.530
Gavi set up facility and Covax by gets all

00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:54.400
of its vaccines from the
serum Institute of India

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:56.510
which has stopped shipping.

00:24:56.510 --> 00:24:57.900
And we don't know when it's gonna resume

00:24:57.900 --> 00:25:01.560
so the lack of vaccines in
the countries where we are

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:04.420
is a massive problem, it's a huge problem.

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:07.500
So for us, going back
to some sort of normal

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:11.930
or some stability is
still not on the cards.

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:15.070
It's not in our vision
because we just cannot

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:18.600
see when our students and
others will be vaccinated.

00:25:18.600 --> 00:25:20.390
The best we've been able to do

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:24.120
is vaccinate some of our
frontline healthcare workers.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:27.010
But other than that this
is an ongoing challenge

00:25:27.010 --> 00:25:29.430
that we will face for
the foreseeable future

00:25:29.430 --> 00:25:32.470
until we see some solution

00:25:32.470 --> 00:25:34.900
to vaccinations for other countries

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:36.930
outside the Western world.

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:40.700
And we see solutions that
we figure out how to live

00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:43.530
with COVID in all of
its different variants

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:46.610
and manifestations over
the next period of time.

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:47.680
I'll stop here now

00:25:48.920 --> 00:25:50.020
- That's a really good point you raised

00:25:50.020 --> 00:25:51.230
and if I may comment on that

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:53.969
as someone who has
spent most of his career

00:25:53.969 --> 00:25:58.600
on vaccine research, I think we,

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:01.810
as a world we have to do better

00:26:01.810 --> 00:26:04.540
in terms of getting the
vaccines out to countries

00:26:04.540 --> 00:26:08.620
where the outbreak is not
gonna be over in a few months,

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:11.480
the prospects of things
getting under control

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:13.750
for higher education, for our countries

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:18.750
like the US are high, even
with the variants, et cetera.

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:21.270
But with the inequity
in vaccine distribution

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.470
is going to impact, continue
to impact higher education

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:27.500
and the broader society going forward.

00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:31.720
So I think as academics as
institutions and as students

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:34.770
I know that a bunch of
students that usually

00:26:34.770 --> 00:26:38.990
sort of watch our events
in this conversation series

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:41.300
it is our responsibility to be advocates

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:43.700
and problem solvers with our partners

00:26:43.700 --> 00:26:45.820
in low and middle income countries.

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:49.230
Switching to another sort
of round of questions,

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:52.920
Peter, what changes have occurred

00:26:52.920 --> 00:26:55.390
that will strengthen the institutions

00:26:55.390 --> 00:26:58.980
like Yale moving forward, particularly,

00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:01.203
how it engages with the rest of the world.

00:27:02.190 --> 00:27:05.043
- Yeah, thank you for that question,

00:27:06.940 --> 00:27:08.670
first of all, I would say

00:27:08.670 --> 00:27:13.670
that our worldview has
changed as a result of COVID.

00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:21.140
We more than ever recognize that we have,

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:22.257
we are citizens of the world

00:27:22.257 --> 00:27:25.562
and we have obligations to the world.

00:27:25.562 --> 00:27:30.562
Once again, we are, what COVID
has done is make the question

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:37.000
of disparity of inequity,
so salient to us.

00:27:37.327 --> 00:27:39.720
And I think we can all
know that intellectually

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:44.500
but as the epidemic, as
the pandemic unfolded,

00:27:44.500 --> 00:27:48.400
where, who, what communities
were affected most severely

00:27:49.310 --> 00:27:54.310
what individuals had the
most severe outcomes.

00:27:55.790 --> 00:27:57.660
And now, as we have vaccine

00:27:57.660 --> 00:27:59.330
where is the vaccine being distributed

00:27:59.330 --> 00:28:00.910
and where isn't it, right?

00:28:00.910 --> 00:28:04.600
So we as institutions of higher learning

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:09.420
we have to recognize
that we can play a role

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:14.420
in promoting inequalities,
promoting disparities in health

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:19.590
or we can play a role where
we're part of the solution.

00:28:19.590 --> 00:28:23.050
And I think going forward,
we need to be thinking

00:28:23.050 --> 00:28:26.100
and I know most people
share this view in principle

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:29.790
but we may need to be
thinking about how to do that

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:34.790
how to reduce disparities in
our own society and globally.

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:38.980
Now you asked me the
question, what changes have

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:41.650
occurred that would strengthen
institutions moving forward

00:28:41.650 --> 00:28:44.460
particularly how they engage in the world.

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:48.120
I think we have become much more nimble

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:51.430
as a result of dealing with the pandemic

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:56.430
at working across boundaries
and at collaborating, right?

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:59.190
The value of collaboration
has never been clearer.

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:01.820
And that happens at multiple levels.

00:29:01.820 --> 00:29:05.600
So our administrative structures

00:29:07.180 --> 00:29:10.630
find people no longer in their silos,

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:12.540
no longer not talking to each other

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.310
but working with each other every day.

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:15.910
The number of people who worked,

00:29:16.790 --> 00:29:19.180
who have been working for 14 months

00:29:19.180 --> 00:29:21.310
with someone they didn't even know

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:26.662
prior to the pandemic is huge, right?

00:29:26.662 --> 00:29:28.610
The number of faculty working

00:29:28.610 --> 00:29:31.120
across boundaries and disciplines, right?

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:34.100
So the people modeling the epidemic

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:36.791
working with those developing vaccines,

00:29:36.791 --> 00:29:41.791
working with with economists
about how to deliver vaccines

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:46.810
right, much more integrated
approach to solving a problem.

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:51.040
And of course, a more global perspective

00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:54.360
on how that problem needs to be solved.

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.150
No one person, no institution

00:29:56.150 --> 00:30:00.570
no nation can solve a
crisis like this on its own.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:03.870
I think that the crisis
has really accelerated

00:30:04.790 --> 00:30:07.350
our understanding of
the need to participate

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:11.150
and engage in multidisciplinary teams

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:13.160
and international partnerships.

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:14.570
Can you give me just one example?

00:30:14.570 --> 00:30:18.210
It's a small example, but it's important.

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:21.740
So since the start of the pandemic,

00:30:21.740 --> 00:30:25.460
the results of thousands of
not yet published studies

00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:28.990
from around the world relevant to COVID-19

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:35.810
have been generating right findings

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:38.300
from thousands and thousands of studies

00:30:38.300 --> 00:30:39.823
tens of thousands probably.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:42.630
Well, how do we make sure there's a way

00:30:42.630 --> 00:30:46.793
for everybody to benefit
from those findings?

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:52.440
Well, Yale partnered up
with Cold Spring Harbor Labs

00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:57.440
and BMJ actually to create
something called med archive.

00:30:59.746 --> 00:31:04.073
And it's a pre-print server
for health-related research.

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:09.010
And this is a small contribution

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:11.360
but it really allows
scientists to share data

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:13.920
and insights across boundaries

00:31:13.920 --> 00:31:18.920
of discipline across boundaries,
across national boundaries.

00:31:19.010 --> 00:31:24.010
And it can dramatically accelerate
the search for treatment

00:31:24.260 --> 00:31:28.590
the clinical treatments,
the search for vaccines.

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:31.130
And I think it's a good example

00:31:31.130 --> 00:31:35.380
of how do we build networks, platforms,

00:31:35.380 --> 00:31:38.580
ways of working across the world

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:40.640
that break down barriers that historically

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:44.370
have stood in the way of progress.

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:47.400
I think universities
play a particular role

00:31:47.400 --> 00:31:49.940
in their ability to convene

00:31:49.940 --> 00:31:52.463
their ability to create such platforms,

00:31:53.530 --> 00:31:58.530
their ability to work in
a less politicized context

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:04.853
than our governments can with others

00:32:05.750 --> 00:32:08.520
like-minded others around
the world in addressing

00:32:10.450 --> 00:32:12.220
something like a pandemic,

00:32:12.220 --> 00:32:15.483
but other kinds of global crises as well.

00:32:17.410 --> 00:32:19.680
- I think that's a great point,

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:23.650
go moving to, moving on to Betsy.

00:32:23.650 --> 00:32:28.000
So there's been sort of give
you an audience question

00:32:28.000 --> 00:32:31.360
and there's a lot of discussion
of keeping campuses safe

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:35.600
at least in the US and
fall through vaccination.

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:38.180
Do you think requiring students,
and that's the question

00:32:38.180 --> 00:32:39.610
from one of the audience members,

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:40.710
do you think requiring students

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:44.360
to be vaccinated will become
a norm on college campuses,

00:32:44.360 --> 00:32:47.810
at least in countries where
the vaccine is available?

00:32:47.810 --> 00:32:48.820
- Thanks for the question

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:51.170
and I saw that in the Q
and A and nice to see you

00:32:51.170 --> 00:32:52.937
since I know the person who asks,

00:32:52.937 --> 00:32:55.230
I think it's too early to tell

00:32:56.270 --> 00:32:58.760
that certainly is our approach at Vassar

00:32:58.760 --> 00:33:01.280
that students will be
required to be vaccinated,

00:33:01.280 --> 00:33:04.090
however, they will be able to opt out

00:33:04.090 --> 00:33:07.082
if they have medical reasons
or religious reasons,

00:33:07.082 --> 00:33:09.810
there's always a path off

00:33:09.810 --> 00:33:12.670
but we'd like to get ourselves to,

00:33:12.670 --> 00:33:15.970
95, 98% of our students are vaccinated.

00:33:15.970 --> 00:33:19.420
And I think that that is
really an important piece

00:33:19.420 --> 00:33:22.670
and it ties very well
to sort of this larger

00:33:22.670 --> 00:33:25.230
culture of we proceeds me,

00:33:25.230 --> 00:33:27.600
we've worked on that hard at Vassar.

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:30.040
And I do think at this time

00:33:30.040 --> 00:33:33.320
students get that getting
vaccinated is part of that

00:33:33.320 --> 00:33:36.360
Maybe they're nervous about it

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:38.240
but I think at the end of the day

00:33:38.240 --> 00:33:41.500
they're gonna privilege
their more social environment

00:33:41.500 --> 00:33:44.460
than their own individual view on that.

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:47.110
We did in fact, yesterday
have a clinic here

00:33:47.110 --> 00:33:48.970
and vaccinated 450 people.

00:33:48.970 --> 00:33:50.980
So that was really exciting,

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:52.750
again working with the state of New York.

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:57.000
And I hope that we'll be able
to do that over the long haul.

00:33:57.000 --> 00:33:59.960
- I just wanna quickly say,
I completely agree with Betsy

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:04.960
and we need to, we need our
students to be vaccinated.

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:07.760
We're gonna, we're
working very hard to get

00:34:07.760 --> 00:34:12.310
vaccine available to them in an easy way.

00:34:12.310 --> 00:34:15.060
And it's probably too early to answer

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:17.920
the mandatory question,
but I definitely lean

00:34:17.920 --> 00:34:19.823
in that direction quite strongly.

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:22.290
- Great.
- Good to hear that.

00:34:22.290 --> 00:34:25.480
- So I'm so glad as a vaccine researcher

00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:27.720
and that someone who has
contributed to vaccine policy

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:29.750
I'm particularly happy
to see a bunch of vaccine

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:30.960
related questions.

00:34:30.960 --> 00:34:33.000
So the next question is to Firoz

00:34:34.750 --> 00:34:38.983
especially given sort of a
huge role in policy, et cetera,

00:34:39.940 --> 00:34:44.010
how can universities all across,

00:34:44.010 --> 00:34:49.010
but deal with misconceptions
regarding vaccines

00:34:50.704 --> 00:34:54.420
around the world in
countries where you work

00:34:55.260 --> 00:34:59.530
and how can institutions
of higher education,

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:02.579
especially with a strong health component,

00:35:02.579 --> 00:35:06.860
convince people that these
vaccines are safe and effective.

00:35:06.860 --> 00:35:09.310
This is another audience
questions, as I said, yeah

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:11.720
- Alright, that's a difficult question

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:13.080
is I think at any part of the world,

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:18.080
but certainly for us vaccine
hesitancy or reluctance

00:35:18.170 --> 00:35:21.650
has been a fact of life for many years now

00:35:21.650 --> 00:35:26.650
as you are aware, all the
different types of vaccinations

00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:30.960
for children that require
have been a challenge

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:32.450
for countries like Pakistan

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:35.400
and East Africa polio vaccinations

00:35:35.400 --> 00:35:37.970
they've been a big challenge as you know

00:35:37.970 --> 00:35:39.800
that Pakistan is one of the few countries

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:42.730
that still has cases of polio

00:35:42.730 --> 00:35:47.730
and the vaccinations campaigns
have been mostly successful

00:35:47.880 --> 00:35:50.130
but not entirely successful.

00:35:50.130 --> 00:35:55.130
And the reasons vary
from social, political,

00:35:57.610 --> 00:36:02.610
clan to I'm not sure I would
agree that in our countries,

00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:05.450
there are religious reasons,

00:36:05.450 --> 00:36:10.450
They may be people who take
advantage of or use religion

00:36:10.700 --> 00:36:13.373
as a reason for people
not to get vaccinated.

00:36:14.430 --> 00:36:19.430
So the issue of vaccine first
for us, it's availability

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:23.200
as we mentioned, once we
have vaccine availability

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:25.510
then we hope that

00:36:25.510 --> 00:36:28.593
we're able to vaccinate as
many people as possible.

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:33.350
The challenge is going to
be access to remote areas

00:36:33.350 --> 00:36:35.430
where our populations live.

00:36:35.430 --> 00:36:37.500
And then once you get
to those remote areas,

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:40.480
working with the clan leaders
and the tribal leaders

00:36:40.480 --> 00:36:42.700
to have them agree that that village

00:36:42.700 --> 00:36:44.700
will be vaccinated or not.

00:36:44.700 --> 00:36:48.910
And vaccinations have had a history

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:52.660
in parts of the world where
we work, which will make it

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:54.480
I think a challenge that we are gonna have

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:57.390
to work with governments,
work with social groups

00:36:57.390 --> 00:37:01.750
work with village communities,
with civil society

00:37:01.750 --> 00:37:05.410
to be able to get people to
become much more comfortable

00:37:05.410 --> 00:37:09.630
that they will be able to accept vaccines.

00:37:09.630 --> 00:37:13.700
The issue also as you know
Saad as a public health expert

00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:16.690
in the first wave in
the second wave perhaps

00:37:16.690 --> 00:37:20.110
Africa and Asia were less effected.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:21.130
If you look at the rates

00:37:21.130 --> 00:37:23.290
and people said it was the demography

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:26.660
much younger population,
it was innate immunity

00:37:26.660 --> 00:37:29.150
because they live with
pathogens all the time.

00:37:29.150 --> 00:37:31.870
I mean, there were lots of
theories that are out there.

00:37:31.870 --> 00:37:34.570
So people have the sense that,

00:37:34.570 --> 00:37:35.890
well it's not gonna affect me

00:37:35.890 --> 00:37:38.090
this is a disease of the West

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:39.960
that's where it's happening.

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:42.530
We, those of us who live
in the developing world

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:46.230
are not really gonna be as
affected as those elsewhere

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:48.000
but that's beginning to change

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:50.020
if you look at what's
happening in India right now.

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:52.840
And if you look at some of the
countries of Africa as well

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:56.370
South Africa being one, Kenya,
which is where we are based

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:59.720
we can see a massive
surge in the third wave.

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:02.700
And so obviously the
something is changing,

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:04.249
it is a variance or changing it

00:38:04.249 --> 00:38:06.840
or some other factors are changing.

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:11.060
So this smugness, if you like about,

00:38:11.060 --> 00:38:12.690
we don't need the vaccines

00:38:12.690 --> 00:38:14.270
is going to disappear very quickly

00:38:14.270 --> 00:38:18.070
so it's sort of a long
answer to your question

00:38:18.070 --> 00:38:20.190
but I think it's gonna
take multiple inputs

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:23.340
and multiple players and multiple factors

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:25.723
to be able to convince
people to be vaccinated.

00:38:27.450 --> 00:38:29.800
- Great, so the next question is Peter

00:38:29.800 --> 00:38:32.310
I'll ask you the question
and others can chime in

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:36.860
it's from from Dean Sten
Vermund, who's the Dean

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:37.870
for those of you who don't know

00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:40.570
he's the Dean of Yale
school of Public Health,

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:45.330
is a very prominent
public health professional

00:38:45.330 --> 00:38:49.960
and has contributed substantially
to the pandemic response

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:51.230
during this pandemic as well.

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:55.230
So he, obviously his
initial comment is that,

00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:59.030
he congratulates the universities leaders

00:38:59.030 --> 00:39:02.202
for making their respective
communities safe,

00:39:02.202 --> 00:39:05.270
but his question is that there were a lot

00:39:05.270 --> 00:39:10.270
of parallel efforts that
happened at each campus.

00:39:10.880 --> 00:39:15.880
Is there a way to come
up with SOP approaches

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:22.350
that can be shared across
different universities,

00:39:22.350 --> 00:39:24.280
at least different
classes of universities,

00:39:24.280 --> 00:39:26.320
different categories of universities

00:39:26.320 --> 00:39:28.450
based on their size, location

00:39:28.450 --> 00:39:31.790
and the composition of
student body, et cetera.

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:33.830
So maybe Peter, you can start

00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:36.773
and if others wanna chime
in that'd be great as well.

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:39.440
- Sten, I thank you for that question.

00:39:39.440 --> 00:39:44.440
And I think there are
informal efforts to do that

00:39:44.470 --> 00:39:47.164
but I think the general goal

00:39:47.164 --> 00:39:52.164
of trying to write the
case study and develop

00:39:52.930 --> 00:39:55.440
the standard operating procedures

00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:59.163
for the future is a very, very good one.

00:40:00.420 --> 00:40:03.990
I'm part of a few of the
networks that Yale is a part of.

00:40:03.990 --> 00:40:07.360
So the Ivy League presidents get together

00:40:07.360 --> 00:40:09.070
on a pretty regular basis.

00:40:09.070 --> 00:40:11.210
And there's an incredible openness

00:40:11.210 --> 00:40:15.830
about sharing our experiences with COVID

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:22.290
the AAU which is a group of
university research universities

00:40:22.330 --> 00:40:27.330
it's primarily US and
that group gets together

00:40:31.770 --> 00:40:36.503
and talks among itself
sharing information.

00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:41.220
So I think that'd be great to step back

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:45.699
and try to write a
playbook for the next time

00:40:45.699 --> 00:40:48.510
there's certain it's
very interesting, right?

00:40:48.510 --> 00:40:51.680
Cause there's certainly
lots of general principles

00:40:51.680 --> 00:40:53.200
that we can develop.

00:40:53.200 --> 00:40:56.753
I think one of them is
listening to experts,

00:40:59.590 --> 00:41:00.690
respecting science

00:41:00.690 --> 00:41:02.250
I'm thinking at the most
general level, right?

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:04.620
Listening to experts, respecting science

00:41:04.620 --> 00:41:09.620
acting congruently with
for us the guidance

00:41:09.660 --> 00:41:13.030
from the state of Connecticut,
guidance from the CDC.

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:17.360
Having said that there also
is a lot of local variability.

00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:18.890
Betsy was giving the example

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:22.745
they are in a fairly
rural part of the state.

00:41:22.745 --> 00:41:25.470
I mean, there's an
important town near them,

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:27.020
- Wrong, careful.

00:41:27.020 --> 00:41:31.230
- You have a small state,
you have a small city

00:41:31.230 --> 00:41:34.330
and then a larger rural catchment
area, I guess I would say.

00:41:34.330 --> 00:41:38.550
But every campus is a little
different is all I'm saying

00:41:38.550 --> 00:41:41.650
and even when I talked to
the Ivy League presidents

00:41:41.650 --> 00:41:44.550
what can be done, let me
not make it so personal.

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:46.570
What could be done at Dartmouth

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:49.750
sitting in Hanover, New
Hampshire was very different

00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:52.460
than what could be done at Columbia

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:54.770
sitting on the upper
West side of Manhattan,

00:41:54.770 --> 00:41:58.140
just to use more more pointed examples

00:41:58.140 --> 00:41:59.870
or more obvious example.

00:41:59.870 --> 00:42:01.720
So yes, I think we can write

00:42:01.720 --> 00:42:04.060
the standard operating procedures.

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:07.930
And I think step one is going
to be listen to our experts

00:42:07.930 --> 00:42:10.640
listen to our scientists,
respect the modelers

00:42:12.604 --> 00:42:15.520
and the like but then
there is going to have

00:42:15.520 --> 00:42:19.503
to be some variability
across institutions too.

00:42:22.110 --> 00:42:27.110
- Great, that's I think
that's an important

00:42:27.320 --> 00:42:29.340
set of principles for perhaps

00:42:30.960 --> 00:42:34.780
other crises as well, I
guess, other than pandemics

00:42:34.780 --> 00:42:36.633
and so on and so forth,

00:42:36.633 --> 00:42:41.633
Betsy, switching to
international collaborations,

00:42:41.713 --> 00:42:45.440
and it's sort of, any
thoughts on how COVID pandemic

00:42:45.440 --> 00:42:48.600
has played a role in
connecting universities

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:53.370
around the world and how
universities can effectively

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:55.930
and strategically create
learning networks.

00:42:55.930 --> 00:42:59.260
So that's another question
from our audience.

00:42:59.260 --> 00:43:01.940
- Yeah, and I agree with
what Peter had just said

00:43:01.940 --> 00:43:03.950
about local networks being so important

00:43:03.950 --> 00:43:06.160
but let's take it to the higher level.

00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:07.390
What I've sort of witnessed

00:43:07.390 --> 00:43:09.870
during this pandemic is the, and again

00:43:09.870 --> 00:43:12.060
this is very much reflective of Vassar

00:43:12.060 --> 00:43:16.600
and Vassar sort of deep
commitment to the liberal arts

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:18.180
and multidisciplinary thinking

00:43:18.180 --> 00:43:19.660
and sort of critical thinking

00:43:19.660 --> 00:43:23.840
what I've really noticed is
universities around the world

00:43:23.840 --> 00:43:26.640
that are just dipping their toes

00:43:26.640 --> 00:43:28.980
into what really is liberal arts.

00:43:28.980 --> 00:43:31.890
And particularly I'm
thinking of the university

00:43:31.890 --> 00:43:34.110
of Global Health Equity in Rwanda,

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:36.050
which I started working
with when I was at Yale,

00:43:36.050 --> 00:43:38.440
but we continue to at Vassar

00:43:38.440 --> 00:43:40.630
or the university of Liberia,

00:43:40.630 --> 00:43:43.330
these are places that are really working

00:43:43.330 --> 00:43:45.210
to develop a medical care system

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:48.040
and a healthcare system that
can prevent future pandemics.

00:43:48.040 --> 00:43:49.360
And what are they asking for?

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:53.500
They're asking for some
courses that prepare people

00:43:53.500 --> 00:43:55.110
in the areas of the liberal arts

00:43:55.110 --> 00:43:56.860
meaning critical thinking humanities,

00:43:56.860 --> 00:43:59.610
thinking about it's not
just the medical approach

00:43:59.610 --> 00:44:02.190
to the vaccination and
the dealing with COVID

00:44:02.190 --> 00:44:05.590
but the thinking more
broadly about humanity

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:09.460
and how history can teach
us how we might want to

00:44:09.460 --> 00:44:10.970
deal with these things in the future.

00:44:10.970 --> 00:44:14.630
So what I find interesting
is whether we will ultimately

00:44:14.630 --> 00:44:18.540
have more international
collaborations where we're trading

00:44:18.540 --> 00:44:21.210
what each other knows
in the case of Vassar

00:44:21.210 --> 00:44:23.100
I think we're very well versed in the area

00:44:23.100 --> 00:44:25.670
of liberal arts and the
pedagogy that goes with that.

00:44:25.670 --> 00:44:28.260
And I actually think
the pandemic has shown

00:44:28.260 --> 00:44:30.220
how important those sort of out of the box

00:44:30.220 --> 00:44:32.513
thinking skills are the same time,

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:36.670
as Peter said, we're in
one place in New York

00:44:36.670 --> 00:44:39.490
and it also we learn a
lot from being engaged

00:44:39.490 --> 00:44:41.880
internationally of what what others may

00:44:42.730 --> 00:44:45.680
maybe prioritizing, et
cetera, so I think it's opened

00:44:45.680 --> 00:44:47.210
up opportunities to tell you the truth.

00:44:47.210 --> 00:44:48.620
Plus we're all used to zoom now,

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:50.930
so it's so much easier to have conferences

00:44:50.930 --> 00:44:54.570
and just this event, it
was a 10th of the price

00:44:54.570 --> 00:44:56.880
that normally would be to
fly everybody in and whatnot.

00:44:56.880 --> 00:44:59.710
So I think we're really galvanizing

00:44:59.710 --> 00:45:01.590
that kind of collaboration internationally

00:45:01.590 --> 00:45:03.090
and it will amplify over time.

00:45:05.429 --> 00:45:10.429
- Wonderful, Firoz, AKU played a role

00:45:10.790 --> 00:45:15.340
a very interesting role in
quickly building capacity

00:45:15.340 --> 00:45:18.080
in healthcare providers in countries

00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:22.720
where it will work so through, for example

00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:24.380
building capacity for critical care

00:45:24.380 --> 00:45:29.380
or sort of patients who
require critical care,

00:45:29.740 --> 00:45:34.700
even when it's not available
and quickly up scaling,

00:45:34.700 --> 00:45:38.250
scaling gap undergraduate
education online, et cetera.

00:45:38.250 --> 00:45:40.140
So any interesting perspective

00:45:40.140 --> 00:45:42.980
of using these online platforms

00:45:42.980 --> 00:45:45.830
that can outlast the pandemic

00:45:46.770 --> 00:45:49.000
that were built up fairly quickly

00:45:49.000 --> 00:45:52.330
both in terms of the
university's core mission

00:45:52.330 --> 00:45:56.870
but as its expanded mission
to build capacity in a crisis,

00:45:56.870 --> 00:45:59.250
especially in a situation
where internet access

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:00.993
is not uniformly available.

00:46:01.930 --> 00:46:04.080
- So two parts to your
question, so the first one

00:46:04.080 --> 00:46:06.470
I would just want to
broaden it a little bit

00:46:06.470 --> 00:46:09.470
and maybe pick up on the
international collaboration

00:46:09.470 --> 00:46:11.230
question you pass on to Betsy

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:14.440
in parts of the world where we are Saad

00:46:14.440 --> 00:46:18.560
one of the lessons for us
during COVID was the following.

00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:20.990
As soon as the pandemic hit

00:46:20.990 --> 00:46:24.260
we couldn't buy PPEs, cause
countries stopped exporting them

00:46:24.260 --> 00:46:26.810
there were rules that
said you can't export

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:28.170
you couldn't buy therapeutics

00:46:28.170 --> 00:46:30.230
because countries were stopping people

00:46:30.230 --> 00:46:34.360
their companies from exporting
it, you can't buy vaccines.

00:46:34.360 --> 00:46:37.600
And we realized in the developing world

00:46:37.600 --> 00:46:41.260
we were at the mercy of
the generosity of others

00:46:41.260 --> 00:46:45.170
and the international, the
WHOs and other systems,

00:46:45.170 --> 00:46:48.460
Gavi, Covax while well-meaning

00:46:48.460 --> 00:46:50.650
had limited ability to make a difference

00:46:50.650 --> 00:46:52.490
in what I just described.

00:46:52.490 --> 00:46:54.730
So it became very clear
to us very quickly.

00:46:54.730 --> 00:46:57.320
We are going to have
to build local capacity

00:46:57.320 --> 00:47:00.720
to be able in research in therapeutics

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:04.980
in understanding treatments
that work locally

00:47:04.980 --> 00:47:07.150
but that autonomy that independence

00:47:07.150 --> 00:47:10.390
from all the reliance
on the lack of reliance

00:47:10.390 --> 00:47:12.980
from other international players

00:47:12.980 --> 00:47:16.070
was something that came
hit home really hard.

00:47:16.070 --> 00:47:17.377
And this is something
we are thinking about

00:47:17.377 --> 00:47:20.797
and it will shape what this
university, our university

00:47:20.797 --> 00:47:23.440
and other universities
in parts of the world,

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:25.660
where we are what they decide to do

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:29.620
and where they decide to put
their focus as we go forward.

00:47:29.620 --> 00:47:33.150
As far as the issue of, as you said,

00:47:33.150 --> 00:47:35.950
going to online learning in countries,

00:47:35.950 --> 00:47:37.170
where there is a challenge

00:47:37.170 --> 00:47:39.610
of the availability of the internet.

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:42.270
We've pivoted very quickly,
we were very fortunate,

00:47:42.270 --> 00:47:44.943
we started a teaching and learning center

00:47:44.943 --> 00:47:47.960
or network very early

00:47:47.960 --> 00:47:50.920
it started to look at hybrid learning,

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:52.940
blended learning, asynchronous learning

00:47:54.101 --> 00:47:57.230
because of the sheer number
of countries we were in

00:47:57.230 --> 00:47:59.690
and the dispersion of our student body

00:47:59.690 --> 00:48:01.730
and faculty for that matter.

00:48:01.730 --> 00:48:04.410
So we had already started
to train our faculty

00:48:04.410 --> 00:48:07.150
in thinking about this,
but when the pandemic hit

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:10.090
it was a very sudden, I think Peter used

00:48:10.090 --> 00:48:12.930
the word nimble, agile
would be other words,

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:16.010
we had to be extremely agile to switch

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:19.550
and start teaching our faculty
about creating materials

00:48:19.550 --> 00:48:22.060
that they were using for
face-to-face teaching,

00:48:22.060 --> 00:48:23.720
for online learning.

00:48:23.720 --> 00:48:25.380
And the population of students

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:27.040
didn't know how to learn online,

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:28.430
they'd never done it before.

00:48:28.430 --> 00:48:30.010
So we not only had to train the faculty

00:48:30.010 --> 00:48:31.470
we had to train the students

00:48:31.470 --> 00:48:34.010
and the students many of whom were remote

00:48:34.010 --> 00:48:37.480
either had limited internet
access or intermittent.

00:48:37.480 --> 00:48:40.970
And the one thing that in
the West we take for granted

00:48:40.970 --> 00:48:44.010
that we don't have in our
parts of the world is power.

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:45.660
We didn't have electricity.

00:48:45.660 --> 00:48:46.980
So why we said to the students

00:48:46.980 --> 00:48:48.650
you need to go home and we'll,

00:48:48.650 --> 00:48:51.220
you learn from home and they would,

00:48:51.220 --> 00:48:53.130
there was intermittent electricity.

00:48:53.130 --> 00:48:57.150
So all of these challenges
are continuing for us,

00:48:57.150 --> 00:48:59.500
and within that, we are
trying to make this work.

00:48:59.500 --> 00:49:02.950
Now we were able to give devices to,

00:49:02.950 --> 00:49:05.860
because of donations and
generosity of people,

00:49:05.860 --> 00:49:08.440
to our students so they
could actually have a device

00:49:08.440 --> 00:49:11.110
like a notepad or a computer

00:49:11.110 --> 00:49:13.420
from through which they could access.

00:49:13.420 --> 00:49:16.800
And that was as basic as
that we had to deal with.

00:49:16.800 --> 00:49:18.610
But I'm happy to say that when you look

00:49:18.610 --> 00:49:21.682
at the results of our
students after 14 months

00:49:21.682 --> 00:49:22.989
although they didn't,

00:49:22.989 --> 00:49:27.140
the academic year was
longer cause we had to stop.

00:49:27.140 --> 00:49:30.400
We are encouraged by
the results that we see

00:49:30.400 --> 00:49:32.660
although Peter's point is very important,

00:49:32.660 --> 00:49:34.260
it's not the same.

00:49:34.260 --> 00:49:37.010
We are seeing that the
experience is not the same

00:49:37.010 --> 00:49:39.500
of people who are learning online versus

00:49:39.500 --> 00:49:44.050
in among classmates, among
others, among faculty.

00:49:44.050 --> 00:49:46.170
And so that is something
we are trying to figure out

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:48.480
how we can create that environment.

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:51.500
So people feel that the
experiential learning

00:49:51.500 --> 00:49:54.473
is an equally important part of content

00:49:54.473 --> 00:49:57.553
that we see that comes
through online learning.

00:50:00.160 --> 00:50:03.240
- We have another
question from the audience

00:50:04.370 --> 00:50:06.940
and it's about teaching
and learning centers.

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:09.570
And Peter Poorvu Center at Yale

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:11.810
have played a hugely important role

00:50:11.810 --> 00:50:14.220
in sustaining our activities

00:50:14.220 --> 00:50:15.980
our quality of education, et cetera

00:50:15.980 --> 00:50:20.120
but going forward and thinking,
building on our experience

00:50:20.120 --> 00:50:24.380
and this question comes from
professor Tashman Cormen

00:50:24.380 --> 00:50:27.540
who is a leader in curriculum development

00:50:27.540 --> 00:50:30.603
and quality in teaching,
et cetera, outside Yale.

00:50:31.510 --> 00:50:36.510
And so what lessons can we share

00:50:38.260 --> 00:50:41.120
in terms of the value of
teaching and learning centers

00:50:41.120 --> 00:50:44.483
not just in pandemics, et
cetera, but beyond that as well.

00:50:45.840 --> 00:50:48.370
- Sure, we have a wonderful teaching

00:50:48.370 --> 00:50:50.790
and learning center at Yale
called the Poorvu Center.

00:50:50.790 --> 00:50:55.790
And in a matter of two week
spring break, last March,

00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:03.120
they offered classes
small group workshops,

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:05.500
and one-on-one tutorials

00:51:05.500 --> 00:51:08.930
to essentially all the faculty teaching,

00:51:08.930 --> 00:51:11.180
especially Yale college students

00:51:13.480 --> 00:51:15.740
and getting them comfortable with zoom

00:51:15.740 --> 00:51:17.490
and teaching on technology.

00:51:17.490 --> 00:51:19.210
We weren't optimizing at that point,

00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:20.620
we were just trying to help people

00:51:20.620 --> 00:51:23.840
make the transition quickly,

00:51:23.840 --> 00:51:25.780
but what has happened over time

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:29.750
as both people have
become more comfortable

00:51:29.750 --> 00:51:33.970
is a lot of creativity
around teaching online.

00:51:33.970 --> 00:51:36.820
And again, these teaching
and learning centers

00:51:36.820 --> 00:51:38.770
can be a source of best practice

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:44.630
around collecting these innovations.

00:51:44.940 --> 00:51:47.180
So I have a brother who teaches theater

00:51:47.180 --> 00:51:50.270
he teaches acting online and he said,

00:51:50.270 --> 00:51:52.690
well, I teach acting for the stage,

00:51:52.690 --> 00:51:55.420
but actually zoom lets
me talk a little bit

00:51:55.420 --> 00:51:57.606
about what happens when you're in front

00:51:57.606 --> 00:52:00.400
of a camera and television
acting, film acting, right.

00:52:00.400 --> 00:52:02.660
So he adopted, he changed.

00:52:02.660 --> 00:52:05.940
My wife teaches maternal and child health

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:10.940
actually at a state
university here in our region.

00:52:12.007 --> 00:52:16.090
And there, she had the challenges

00:52:16.090 --> 00:52:19.150
of students who didn't all have broadband

00:52:19.150 --> 00:52:21.986
students all didn't have easy

00:52:21.986 --> 00:52:26.430
and comfortable and isolating
learning environments

00:52:26.430 --> 00:52:31.430
and learn to change her classroom teaching

00:52:32.580 --> 00:52:35.320
in a way that kept them involved

00:52:35.320 --> 00:52:40.320
created more active learning
so that the passivity

00:52:40.520 --> 00:52:42.200
of not having your classmates around you

00:52:42.200 --> 00:52:45.050
or the lack of social facilitation,

00:52:45.050 --> 00:52:46.570
didn't weigh too heavily.

00:52:46.570 --> 00:52:49.030
So our teaching and learning
centers can be wonderful

00:52:49.030 --> 00:52:51.650
repositories of collecting knowledge

00:52:51.650 --> 00:52:54.060
about what works and what doesn't

00:52:54.060 --> 00:52:56.800
and then be great
resource for our faculty.

00:52:56.800 --> 00:53:01.800
Just one more point while teaching online,

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:05.030
teaching through zoom, teaching remotely

00:53:06.290 --> 00:53:10.810
in my view will never
replace classroom instruction

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:15.150
while places like Yale
and places like Vassar

00:53:15.150 --> 00:53:20.150
will continue to be oriented
around in-person instruction

00:53:20.700 --> 00:53:24.820
primarily boy, did they
create leverage for all of us

00:53:24.820 --> 00:53:29.070
boy do they create the
potential for greater reach?

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:32.360
I'm a psychologist and
there's an expression

00:53:32.360 --> 00:53:35.100
that psychologists like to
use came from a president

00:53:35.100 --> 00:53:37.310
of the American psychological association

00:53:37.310 --> 00:53:41.260
which is how do we give psychology away?

00:53:41.260 --> 00:53:44.600
Well, I think a lot about how do we give

00:53:46.180 --> 00:53:48.670
without compromising our mission,

00:53:48.670 --> 00:53:51.800
without compromising our financial health

00:53:51.800 --> 00:53:55.650
how do we give more of Yale away?

00:53:55.650 --> 00:53:58.160
And we can give more of Yale away through,

00:53:58.160 --> 00:54:01.427
the magic of zoom and
we do, and we'll just

00:54:04.860 --> 00:54:09.120
in the kinds of situations
that Firoz mentioned.

00:54:09.120 --> 00:54:12.130
Just one other quick
thought, how are we all gonna

00:54:12.130 --> 00:54:14.930
do our work differently
now that we have become

00:54:14.930 --> 00:54:16.840
now that we as administrators have become

00:54:16.840 --> 00:54:19.430
more comfortable on on zoom

00:54:19.430 --> 00:54:23.440
is my carbon footprint
going to be as large

00:54:23.440 --> 00:54:26.230
with all the traveling that
university presidents do

00:54:27.350 --> 00:54:30.030
traditionally is it going to be as large

00:54:30.030 --> 00:54:33.760
as it once was when I can get together

00:54:33.760 --> 00:54:36.580
with Firoz and Betsy for a meeting,

00:54:36.580 --> 00:54:41.010
on zoom without driving to Poughkeepsie

00:54:41.010 --> 00:54:46.010
or flying to Pakistan or
Kenya, or what have you, right.

00:54:46.830 --> 00:54:48.100
I did grow all, get it,

00:54:48.100 --> 00:54:50.400
I think we're gonna do business
in a lot of different ways.

00:54:50.400 --> 00:54:51.900
And our teaching and learning centers

00:54:51.900 --> 00:54:54.670
are gonna be the one of the places

00:54:54.670 --> 00:54:57.740
that picks up on helping
us figure that out

00:54:57.740 --> 00:55:00.100
not part of their traditional mission

00:55:00.100 --> 00:55:03.610
but they're gonna help us
figure out when it works,

00:55:03.610 --> 00:55:04.443
when it doesn't.

00:55:06.340 --> 00:55:09.360
- So a couple of sort
of last brief questions

00:55:09.360 --> 00:55:12.330
from the audience starting with Betsy.

00:55:12.330 --> 00:55:13.530
I think there was a very interesting

00:55:13.530 --> 00:55:15.360
and thought provoking question about

00:55:15.360 --> 00:55:18.430
physical challenges in
accessing healthcare

00:55:18.430 --> 00:55:21.270
and what institutions have learned

00:55:21.270 --> 00:55:23.683
in this a crisis situation,

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:28.130
about people helping people with

00:55:28.130 --> 00:55:32.330
that have trouble accessing
their surrounding communities,

00:55:32.330 --> 00:55:34.440
et cetera in the healthcare setting,

00:55:34.440 --> 00:55:35.490
Betsy you have thought about

00:55:35.490 --> 00:55:36.880
and worked on these kinds
of issues for a while.

00:55:36.880 --> 00:55:38.770
- Yeah, I think it's an
incredibly interesting

00:55:38.770 --> 00:55:41.800
and important and unexpected that for us,

00:55:41.800 --> 00:55:46.400
the pandemic made all
of us unable to access

00:55:46.400 --> 00:55:48.700
what we used to be able
to access so easily.

00:55:48.700 --> 00:55:52.070
And in a way, I think it
really leveled in some ways

00:55:52.070 --> 00:55:55.163
leveled some playing
fields around the campus.

00:55:56.150 --> 00:55:58.760
There wasn't walking a lot
of classes could be online,

00:55:58.760 --> 00:56:01.060
people could access that better

00:56:01.060 --> 00:56:02.743
closed caption on everything.

00:56:03.680 --> 00:56:05.970
We worked on see-through masks.

00:56:05.970 --> 00:56:08.720
So people who couldn't hear
could see the lips still

00:56:08.720 --> 00:56:11.940
and it was, it has been sort
of an amazing experience

00:56:11.940 --> 00:56:16.640
because I think now
everybody understands better

00:56:16.640 --> 00:56:18.810
what disability could be

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:22.600
and what it could in
fact result in your life.

00:56:22.600 --> 00:56:25.230
As a result we just started
a very high level committee

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:28.190
on accessibility and are
starting to put together

00:56:28.190 --> 00:56:31.940
a much different approach of
how do we make our environment

00:56:31.940 --> 00:56:33.227
accessible for everybody.

00:56:33.227 --> 00:56:35.830
And I never would've thought
the pandemic did this

00:56:35.830 --> 00:56:39.350
but again, it brought us sort
of, we proceeds me approach.

00:56:39.350 --> 00:56:42.370
It made us, I think, more
understanding of each other

00:56:42.370 --> 00:56:44.650
and our different abilities,

00:56:44.650 --> 00:56:46.940
so I'm hopeful for the future on this.

00:56:46.940 --> 00:56:49.120
- So Firoz I'll give you
the last brief question,

00:56:49.120 --> 00:56:52.110
which is what is the future
of international collaborator

00:56:52.110 --> 00:56:56.000
collaborations for this pandemic,
which is not nearly over

00:56:56.000 --> 00:56:57.880
in many parts of the world.

00:56:57.880 --> 00:57:00.560
It's not over in any part
of the world, but it will

00:57:00.560 --> 00:57:03.520
it is likely to go on longer
in many parts of the world

00:57:03.520 --> 00:57:07.063
and beyond what is the
future of collaboration?

00:57:09.830 --> 00:57:11.723
- I think the pandemic has shown us

00:57:11.723 --> 00:57:14.283
that this is not a local situation.

00:57:15.490 --> 00:57:17.000
Something that happens in one part

00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:20.750
of the world now gets traveled
very quickly to another.

00:57:20.750 --> 00:57:22.230
And that's become pretty obvious

00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:24.790
to the I think everybody involved.

00:57:24.790 --> 00:57:28.460
I think universities have a
very important role to play

00:57:28.460 --> 00:57:32.700
in terms of bringing
together people to start to

00:57:32.700 --> 00:57:36.220
address some of these
problems, which are now common.

00:57:36.220 --> 00:57:38.872
We all, that's what we have seen.

00:57:38.872 --> 00:57:41.860
I don't think our governments
have set a good example

00:57:41.860 --> 00:57:44.150
as I said earlier so far.

00:57:44.150 --> 00:57:46.510
And so I think we need to work

00:57:46.510 --> 00:57:49.300
beyond what the governments do not within

00:57:49.300 --> 00:57:51.340
and or wait for leadership
from the governments

00:57:51.340 --> 00:57:52.570
because they have other priorities.

00:57:52.570 --> 00:57:57.210
They have other constituencies
that they have to satisfy

00:57:57.210 --> 00:58:01.150
but I think there is an
even more opportunity now

00:58:01.150 --> 00:58:05.440
for collaborations on
vaccines, on new medications,

00:58:05.440 --> 00:58:09.050
on treatment and in some
ways the pandemic has leveled

00:58:09.050 --> 00:58:10.880
the playing field that
you use the expression

00:58:10.880 --> 00:58:13.069
that Betsy just described.

00:58:13.069 --> 00:58:16.430
We have done clinical to
undertake in clinical trials

00:58:16.430 --> 00:58:20.460
in Pakistan and Kenya,
on Chinese vaccines,

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:24.890
on other vaccines, French
vaccines and so on.

00:58:24.890 --> 00:58:28.360
And we've contributed to the data

00:58:28.360 --> 00:58:31.540
on phase three trials and
understanding of certain

00:58:31.540 --> 00:58:34.150
populations or certain ethnic groups

00:58:34.150 --> 00:58:36.990
and so that's certainly been
part of our contribution

00:58:36.990 --> 00:58:40.570
and no reason why we can't also work

00:58:40.570 --> 00:58:44.500
with other institutions in
developing common standards

00:58:44.500 --> 00:58:47.920
common solutions and common areas

00:58:47.920 --> 00:58:50.320
joint research is beginning to flow.

00:58:50.320 --> 00:58:51.970
And the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation

00:58:51.970 --> 00:58:54.440
has been very strong in supporting this

00:58:54.440 --> 00:58:57.620
and we are recipients of their grants

00:58:57.620 --> 00:58:58.580
and we're very grateful

00:58:58.580 --> 00:59:00.380
for some of the work that they support,

00:59:00.380 --> 00:59:01.460
but we're not the only ones.

00:59:01.460 --> 00:59:05.080
And working with the
University of Michigan

00:59:05.080 --> 00:59:08.280
or University of Washington
or doing work in these areas

00:59:08.280 --> 00:59:12.363
I think we began to demonstrate
how much more it's possible.

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:15.857
- Yeah, and Yale.

00:59:15.857 --> 00:59:18.780
- And Yale, I should just
say while I have a minute

00:59:18.780 --> 00:59:21.300
that our first collaboration
was with the Yale

00:59:21.300 --> 00:59:23.740
I don't know, the hospital,
the New Haven Hospital

00:59:23.740 --> 00:59:25.970
when we started, they
were describing to us how

00:59:25.970 --> 00:59:29.010
they were dealing with this
because we were 60 days after

00:59:29.010 --> 00:59:32.960
in terms of our search
that happened in New Haven.

00:59:32.960 --> 00:59:35.430
And they really helped us understanding.

00:59:35.430 --> 00:59:37.160
There was one thing they
couldn't help us with

00:59:37.160 --> 00:59:38.940
cause they were, they gave us the idea,

00:59:38.940 --> 00:59:42.020
you need to give bonuses
to healthcare workers

00:59:42.020 --> 00:59:44.510
because of the extra
mile they went, right.

00:59:44.510 --> 00:59:47.180
They were dealing with
COVID situations and so on.

00:59:47.180 --> 00:59:48.290
And we had to explain to them

00:59:48.290 --> 00:59:51.180
that we asked our nurses
to take a cut in salary

00:59:51.180 --> 00:59:53.310
so we could keep the institution afloat.

00:59:53.310 --> 00:59:55.770
So everywhere else we were
able to learn from them,

00:59:55.770 --> 00:59:57.390
but I think they were surprised

00:59:57.390 --> 00:59:59.800
of the commitment of our
people and the mission

00:59:59.800 --> 01:00:02.253
that they actually took
cuts in compensation

01:00:02.253 --> 01:00:05.070
to be able to address the
challenges that faced us,

01:00:05.070 --> 01:00:07.460
so yes, including Yale.

01:00:07.460 --> 01:00:11.080
- Yeah, so I'll just wrap up really nice

01:00:11.080 --> 01:00:15.490
and thoughtful session with a few words

01:00:15.490 --> 01:00:19.100
first to say that we have the next event

01:00:19.100 --> 01:00:22.690
with Dr. Tedros, from
WHO that is coming up

01:00:22.690 --> 01:00:25.500
that's the next event in
the conversation series.

01:00:25.500 --> 01:00:27.980
And then as we leave I think they will

01:00:27.980 --> 01:00:32.800
sort of project the slide
so I would invite everyone

01:00:32.800 --> 01:00:35.230
to connect there and
it's shaping up nicely

01:00:35.230 --> 01:00:37.280
based on our discussion
with his team and his staff.

01:00:37.280 --> 01:00:40.840
And it should be an interesting session,

01:00:40.840 --> 01:00:43.110
but I will the last thing I would say

01:00:43.110 --> 01:00:47.760
is that universities have
a unique role in combating

01:00:47.760 --> 01:00:52.760
and preparing the world for
mass challenges like pandemics

01:00:54.310 --> 01:00:57.670
because pandemics often
happen across generations.

01:00:57.670 --> 01:01:02.220
And so universities survive corporations,

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:03.530
they survive governments

01:01:03.530 --> 01:01:06.480
and sometimes the survive republics,

01:01:06.480 --> 01:01:09.750
just as an example the
top eight corporations

01:01:09.750 --> 01:01:14.750
in the US have their life
is the average life span

01:01:16.110 --> 01:01:20.020
of the current topic is
approximately 60 years.

01:01:20.020 --> 01:01:22.240
And Ivy League, the eight Ivy Leagues

01:01:22.240 --> 01:01:27.240
have on average, I think,
close to 275 years old.

01:01:30.340 --> 01:01:33.950
And so therefore there's
a unique responsibility

01:01:33.950 --> 01:01:36.607
on universities and their faculty

01:01:36.607 --> 01:01:38.900
and their students to learn the lessons

01:01:38.900 --> 01:01:40.980
from these kinds of catastrophic events

01:01:40.980 --> 01:01:43.390
and pass on to the next generation,

01:01:43.390 --> 01:01:46.090
through our research, through our teaching

01:01:46.090 --> 01:01:48.970
and sharing how we advise governments

01:01:48.970 --> 01:01:50.800
how we handle these situations

01:01:50.800 --> 01:01:52.670
both as institutions ourselves,

01:01:52.670 --> 01:01:57.030
but also as incubators of some

01:01:57.030 --> 01:02:00.130
of the best expertise available

01:02:00.130 --> 01:02:02.010
for addressing these problems.

01:02:02.010 --> 01:02:05.399
So to our students, no pressure,

01:02:05.399 --> 01:02:08.870
but to our faculty that it's
a unique responsibility,

01:02:08.870 --> 01:02:13.060
I hope we will live up to
going forward for generations.

01:02:13.060 --> 01:02:15.780
So thank you for, to the panelists,

01:02:15.780 --> 01:02:19.680
thank you for the engaged audience

01:02:19.680 --> 01:02:23.650
we got a ton of questions I
couldn't get to all of them,

01:02:23.650 --> 01:02:25.550
actually not even half of them,

01:02:25.550 --> 01:02:28.293
but I really appreciate
everyone's participation.

01:02:29.210 --> 01:02:30.437
- Thank you, Saad

01:02:30.437 --> 01:02:31.310
- Thank you Saad
- Thank you.

01:02:31.310 --> 01:02:32.390
Thank you very much.

01:02:32.390 --> 01:02:33.590
- Nice to see everyone.

01:02:33.590 --> 01:02:34.423
- Bye-bye

01:02:34.423 --> 01:02:36.153
- Bye-bye.
